| PDF 1 | ![PDF 1 hash extraction](images/01-pdf1-hash-extraction.png) |
| PDF 2 | ![PDF 2 hash extraction](images/02-pdf2-hash-extraction.png) |
| PDF 3 | ![PDF 3 hash extraction](images/03-pdf3-hash-extraction.png) |

### Phase 2 — John the Ripper Setup

John the Ripper was checked on Kali Linux:

```bash
john --version
```

PDF format support was checked with:

```bash
john --list=formats | grep -i pdf
```

The lab output identified the PDF format as supported.

### Phase 3 — Dictionary-Based Password Auditing

The RockYou wordlist was used to test candidate passwords against each supplied PDF hash.

If the wordlist is compressed on Kali Linux, extract it first:

```bash
sudo gzip -dk /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt.gz
```

**PDF 1**

```bash
john --format=pdf \
  --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt \
  hashes/PDF1.hash
```

**PDF 2**

```bash
john --format=pdf \
  --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt \
  hashes/PDF2.hash
```

**PDF 3**

```bash
john --format=pdf \
  --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt \
  hashes/PDF3.hash
```

### Phase 4 — Verify Recovered Passwords

Use the following command for each hash file:

```bash
john --show --format=pdf hashes/PDF1.hash
john --show --format=pdf hashes/PDF2.hash
john --show --format=pdf hashes/PDF3.hash
```

The John the Ripper output confirmed successful recovery with:

```text
1 password hash cracked, 0 left
```

